WEBVTT

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:03.951
CanLab.

00:00:03.951 --> 00:00:09.159
A desktop workstation for reverse-engineering a CAN bus.

00:00:09.159 --> 00:00:12.972
Here is a tour of it, on real recordings.

00:00:14.317 --> 00:00:19.946
This is a real recording from a CANedge logger, loaded
straight from its MDF four file:

00:00:19.946 --> 00:00:24.475
nine thousand six hundred frames across fifty arbitration
identifiers,

00:00:24.475 --> 00:00:27.257
every one of them twenty-nine bit extended.

00:00:27.257 --> 00:00:31.333
Nothing here was simulated and nothing was labelled in
advance.

00:00:32.283 --> 00:00:37.123
The work is grouped into five workspaces rather than sixteen
tabs in a row.

00:00:37.123 --> 00:00:41.770
Capture is what is on the wire, Explore is what it looks like
over time,

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:47.255
Detect is the automatic analysis, Define is where you write
down what you worked out,

00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:49.707
and Bus is everything that talks back.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:53.236
First question on an unknown bus:

00:00:53.236 --> 00:00:54.098
what is it.

00:00:54.098 --> 00:00:59.036
Twenty-nine bit identifiers usually mean a truck running
J1939,

00:00:59.036 --> 00:01:06.481
but these carry data page one in the hundred and twenty-six
thousand range, which is NMEA 2000.

00:01:06.481 --> 00:01:07.656
This is a boat.

00:01:07.656 --> 00:01:12.202
Vessel heading, rate of turn, wind, satellite positioning.

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:19.022
Which the decoded fields then confirm, from two messages that
have no reason to agree unless

00:01:19.022 --> 00:01:20.426
the layouts are right.

00:01:20.426 --> 00:01:26.107
Position puts the vessel at forty-two point six six north,
eighty-one point two one west,

00:01:26.107 --> 00:01:27.320
which is Lake Erie.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.065
Heading reads one point nine eight radians,

00:01:30.065 --> 00:01:35.362
and the magnetic variation beside it reads nought point one
five five radians west,

00:01:35.362 --> 00:01:37.533
which is eight point nine degrees:

00:01:37.533 --> 00:01:39.958
the published value for exactly there.

00:01:40.917 --> 00:01:46.400
The frames table is a log, which is the wrong shape for the
question you ask at the bench.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.629
The sniffer collapses the bus to one row per message:

00:01:49.629 --> 00:01:55.173
a byte turns green when it rises and red when it falls, and
bytes that never move stay dim.

00:01:56.117 --> 00:01:58.125
Notch is why this beats scrolling.

00:01:58.125 --> 00:02:02.495
It records every bit that is moving right now and ignores it
from then on,

00:02:02.495 --> 00:02:08.165
so the counters that never stop go quiet and the next thing
that lights up is the thing you did.

00:02:09.117 --> 00:02:14.421
One press masked four hundred and sixty four bits of steady
traffic on this log.

00:02:15.383 --> 00:02:19.409
The detectors run with no knowledge of the protocol at all.

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:24.186
Here they find twenty-four counter bytes across twenty-three
messages,

00:02:24.186 --> 00:02:30.600
twenty-one of them in byte zero, and on five of those the
count wraps at two hundred and fifty

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:30.873
one.

00:02:30.873 --> 00:02:35.923
NMEA 2000 calls byte zero the sequence identifier and
specifies that wrap.

00:02:35.923 --> 00:02:38.447
The detector got there from the data.

00:02:39.383 --> 00:02:43.635
Entropy per bit finds where one field ends and the next
begins.

00:02:43.635 --> 00:02:50.046
Each candidate boundary carries a confidence, which is a match
fraction over the frames loaded,

00:02:50.046 --> 00:02:50.855
not a proof.

00:02:51.817 --> 00:02:55.427
A signal is a message, a start bit, a length and a scale.

00:02:55.427 --> 00:02:59.671
The preview decodes real frames through the definition as you
type,

00:02:59.671 --> 00:03:04.041
so it is checked against the bus rather than against your
arithmetic.

00:03:04.041 --> 00:03:06.764
Nought point seven seven metres per second:

00:03:06.764 --> 00:03:10.945
light air, which is what a moored boat on a calm lake should
read.

00:03:11.883 --> 00:03:14.216
Drag across the grid to claim bits.

00:03:14.216 --> 00:03:20.479
Motorola byte order and non-contiguous layouts go through the
same coordinate map the exporter

00:03:20.479 --> 00:03:23.811
uses, so what you select is what gets written out.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:28.938
Plotted against time is where a definition is confirmed or
refuted.

00:03:28.938 --> 00:03:32.376
Both traces are the same two bytes of the same message.

00:03:32.376 --> 00:03:38.127
The upper one reads them little-endian and wanders between
nought point seven two and nought

00:03:38.127 --> 00:03:40.378
point eight seven metres per second.

00:03:40.378 --> 00:03:46.191
The lower one reads them the other way round and swings
between a hundred and eighty four and

00:03:46.191 --> 00:03:48.004
two hundred and twenty three.

00:03:48.004 --> 00:03:51.942
You do not need the spec to see which of those is a wind
speed.

00:03:52.883 --> 00:03:57.327
Everything that can put a frame on a wire is behind one gate.

00:03:57.327 --> 00:04:02.135
ARM TX is off by default and it covers injection, replay,
fuzzing,

00:04:02.135 --> 00:04:05.631
gateway forwarding and every diagnostic request.

00:04:05.631 --> 00:04:09.419
Disarming stops a running worker within two seconds.

00:04:10.383 --> 00:04:14.110
Before any of that, the page shows the frame it would send.

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:19.100
Byte one holds four D, which is seventy-seven hundredths of a
metre per second,

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.195
and the bytes the signal does not touch stay dim.

00:04:22.195 --> 00:04:27.879
If the vehicle profile writes a counter or a checksum, the
bytes it overwrites turn amber.

00:04:28.817 --> 00:04:31.506
The controls themselves are three rows:

00:04:31.506 --> 00:04:34.265
which signal, what value, and how often.

00:04:34.265 --> 00:04:37.852
The slider spans whatever range the signal declares,

00:04:37.852 --> 00:04:41.921
and every send lands in the log underneath with its result.

00:04:42.883 --> 00:04:44.950
Three things can run alongside:

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:50.882
a REST API, an MCP server that lets an assistant drive the
analysis, and the plugin list.

00:04:50.882 --> 00:04:54.415
None of the twenty-five assistant tools can transmit;

00:04:54.415 --> 00:04:59.347
putting frames on a wire stays behind the gate where a person
is watching.

00:05:00.283 --> 00:05:02.028
Hardware is picked here.

00:05:02.028 --> 00:05:05.664
Every python-can backend is available, plus GVRET,

00:05:05.664 --> 00:05:09.809
which CanLab adds itself for the boards SavvyCAN runs on.

00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:16.498
Detect finds what is plugged in, and Test opens the adapter
and listens for a second without

00:05:16.498 --> 00:05:17.443
transmitting.

00:05:18.383 --> 00:05:21.965
A second recording, and a different vehicle entirely:

00:05:21.965 --> 00:05:25.682
twenty-three minutes off a car, eleven bit identifiers,

00:05:25.682 --> 00:05:28.453
a hundred and fifty five thousand frames.

00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:32.981
One identifier carries a hundred and thirty three thousand of
them.

00:05:32.981 --> 00:05:37.847
The sniffer folds the whole capture into sixteen rows in under
a second.

00:05:38.783 --> 00:05:42.962
Load a capture, work out what the bytes carry, write it down,

00:05:42.962 --> 00:05:45.565
and export a DBC other tools can read.

00:05:45.565 --> 00:05:51.935
Everything shown here ran against real recordings from
hardware this project did not produce.

00:05:53.533 --> 00:05:56.829
CanLab is open source, under the MIT licence.

00:05:56.829 --> 00:06:03.421
The source and the Linux build are on GitHub, and the
documentation is at the second link.
